Among remedies for GNN over-smoothing, current lines of work can be grouped into (1) global / layer-wise normalisation (PairNorm, GraphNorm, ContraNorm-D), (2) residual or coefficient-based schemes that are node specific but depth-invariant (NDLS, PSNR), (3) fixed-filter spectral add-ons that use the same low-, or band-pass kernel for every node (Scattering GCN) and (4) initial-residual identity mapping (GCNII).  
FARN departs from all of them by jointly providing – inside one differentiable layer –
(a) three frequency channels (low-, band-, high-pass) that are computed with polynomial Laplacian filters but **are mixed by every node and at every depth through a learned soft-gating vector**; 
(b) variance-preserving normalisation applied only to the high-frequency branch, thereby decoupling denoising from dimensional collapse; 
(c) a single sparse-matvec cost identical to a standard GCN step (three in parallel) so the mechanism is lightweight and can be dropped into any MPNN without graph rewiring.
No prior paper in the list offers simultaneously node-wise AND layer-wise frequency adaptivity—Scattering GCN is global; NDLS chooses one smoothing depth per node but keeps it fixed across layers and is not end-to-end learned; PSNR adapts residual strength but not spectral content; normalisation-only methods have no spectral control.  The combination of adaptive spectral mixing, residual input preservation, and targeted ContraNorm in one block is therefore genuinely new.
